{
  "slug": "minds-mechanisms",
  "name": "The mind's mechanisms",
  "domain": "Mind",
  "kind": "authored",
  "status": "draft",
  "draftedBy": "Claude, 15 Nov 2026, from Bryan's words the same day: \"sunk cost fallacies cognitive dissonance et cetera ... people don't know how these affect things. These mechanisms are so vital for so much.\" The draft list is docs/MIND-MECHANISMS-draft.md. Every line here waits for Bryan to keep, change or strike.",
  "why": "Your mind runs on shortcuts. Most of the time they help. Some of them cost you money, time and years, and the people selling to you know them better than you do.",
  "outcomes": [
    "Spot the sunk cost trap, and choose by what is ahead instead of what is already spent.",
    "Notice when you are changing the story to stop a clash, instead of changing what you do.",
    "Feel the pull of \"only today\" and \"everyone is going\", and still take the time to choose.",
    "Look for what would prove you wrong, not only for what agrees with you.",
    "Notice when the first number you heard is setting the price, and set your own first.",
    "Weigh a possible loss and an equal gain on the same scale."
  ],
  "units": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "title": "Sunk cost",
      "outcome": "Spot the sunk cost trap, and choose by what is ahead instead of what is already spent.",
      "why": "Money, time and effort you already spent are gone whichever way you choose. Letting them pick for you means paying again for something already lost.",
      "reachesYou": "The car you keep fixing, the job you stay in because of the years you put in, the course you finish though it stopped helping, the plate you clean though you are full.",
      "do": "Pick one thing you keep doing mostly because of what you already put in. Ask: if I were starting fresh today, with nothing spent, would I choose this? Write the answer down.",
      "voices": [
        "thaler",
        "arkes"
      ],
