    {
      "n": 2,
      "title": "Cognitive dissonance",
      "outcome": "Notice when you are changing the story to stop a clash, instead of changing what you do.",
      "why": "When two of your thoughts clash, like \"I chose well\" and \"this is not working\", it feels bad. The fastest way to stop the feeling is to change the story. The fact stays where it was.",
      "reachesYou": "Defending a car, a candidate, a partner or a plan long after it stopped working, because admitting it would mean you chose wrong.",
      "do": "Find one thing you defend a little too hard. Say out loud: \"I chose that, and it is not working.\" Notice the sting, and notice that nothing bad happened.",
      "voices": [
        "festinger",
        "aronson"
      ],
